[0.647, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1;36m# 3b. fixtures: a cluster-set binding, a Placement, and the target namespace\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.002, "o", "  placement demo-all + namespace shop ready\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1;36m# 4. KNOW the fleet - inventory, versions, capacity, add-ons. one question\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[0;90m$ claude -p \"Give me a crisp fleet overview: every managed cluster with availability, Kubernetes version and ...\"\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[22.087, "o", "## Fleet overview\r\n\r\n**3 managed clusters — all healthy, all identical.**\r\n\r\n| Cluster | Available | Joined | Kubernetes | CPU | Memory | ClusterSet |\r\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\r\n| cluster1 | ✅ True | True | v1.30.4 | 4 | ~7.7 Gi | default |\r\n| cluster2 | ✅ True | True | v1.30.4 | 4 | ~7.7 Gi | default |\r\n| cluster3 | ✅ True | True | v1.30.4 | 4 | ~7.7 Gi | default |\r\n\r\n**Fleet capacity:** 12 vCPU / ~23 Gi total. No version skew — every spoke is on v1.30.4.\r\n\r\n**Platform add-ons:** none. Both `ClusterManagementAddOn` (fleet-level definitions) and `ManagedClusterAddOn` (per-cluster instances) returned empty — so there is nothing degraded, but equally nothing installed. This hub is running bare OCM: registration and work agents only, no observability, policy, or search add-ons.\r\n\r\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\r\n- **Two-level add-on model:** `ClusterManagementAddOn` is the hub-side *definition* (what a"]
[0.001, "o", "dd-on exists + its install strategy); `ManagedClusterAddOn` is the per-cluster *instance* living in that cluster's hub namespace. Both empty means no add-on framework is deployed — distinct from \"installed but unhealthy,\" which would show Available=False.\r\n- **`available` vs `joined`:** `joined` is a one-time registration handshake (the klusterlet completed CSR approval); `available` is a live lease heartbeat. A cluster can be joined but unavailable if its agent stops renewing — that's the field to watch for real outages.\r\n- **Capacity is `capacity`, not `allocatable`:** the 4 CPU / 8099496Ki here is total node capacity. Schedulable headroom is lower once kube/system reserved is subtracted — `get_cluster` returns both if you need the real number.\r\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\r\n"]
[0.494, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[0.015, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1;36m# 5. PLACEMENT - where will new workloads land, and why?\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[0;90m$ claude -p \"Explain the placement named demo-all in namespace default: which clusters does it currently sele...\"\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[22.408, "o", "**`demo-all` currently selects `cluster1`, `cluster2`, `cluster3` — the whole fleet.**\r\n\r\nWhy: it binds the `global` ClusterSet (bound in `default` via the `global` ManagedClusterSetBinding), sets no `numberOfClusters` cap, and carries no label/claim predicates — so every accepted, joined cluster in `global` qualifies. All three are `Available=True`, giving 3/3. Status confirms it: `PlacementMisconfigured=False`, `PlacementSatisfied=True`.\r\n\r\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\r\n- The clusters' own label says `clusterset: default`, yet they land in `global` — `global` is OCM's built-in *LabelSelector*-type ClusterSet with an empty selector, so it always contains every ManagedCluster regardless of the per-cluster clusterset label. Binding `global` is the canonical \"select the whole fleet\" idiom.\r\n- The binding is what makes this legal: a Placement can only draw from ClusterSets bound into *its own* namespace. Withou"]
[0.000, "o", "t the `global` binding in `default`, this same Placement would report `PlacementMisconfigured=True` and select zero clusters — a common cause of \"my placement matches nothing.\"\r\n- Note the two-tool split in this MCP server: `list_placements` shows *intent* (spec + conditions), `get_placement_decision` reads the *outcome* from PlacementDecisions. Intent can be satisfiable while decisions lag, so an operator diagnosing drift should always read both.\r\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\r\n"]
[0.511, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[0.015, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1;36m# 6. ship a new service the LAZY way - the guardrails must refuse\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[0;90m$ claude -p \"Ship a new storefront service fast: propose deploying image nginx:latest to namespace shop on cl...\"\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[53.994, "o", "Something refused it. Here's the exact reason, verbatim from the server:\r\n\r\n```\r\nREJECTED by static guardrails:\r\nStatic guardrails rejected this proposal:\r\n- [manifest 0] automountServiceAccountToken must be explicitly false - an auto-mounted API token is a credential the workload does not need.\r\n- [manifest 0] container 'nginx': privileged=true is not allowed.\r\n- [manifest 0] container 'nginx': allowPrivilegeEscalation must be explicitly false.\r\n- [manifest 0] container 'nginx': runAsNonRoot must be true (pod or container).\r\n- [manifest 0] container 'nginx': capabilities.drop must include ALL.\r\n- [manifest 0] container 'nginx': seccompProfile.type must be one of Localhost, RuntimeDefault (pod or container).\r\n- [manifest 0] container 'nginx': image 'nginx:latest' must be pinned (no :latest, no floating tags).\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Verified state:** nothing was applied, and `list_pending_proposals` returns `[]` — the rejected proposal was not even stored for approval. There is no proposal ID to hand anyone, so there is "]
[0.000, "o", "nothing an approver could rubber-stamp later.\r\n\r\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\r\n- **The refusal came from the cheapest layer.** `propose_manifestwork` runs static guardrails first, then a Kyverno dry-run on the hub. This never reached Kyverno — the static pass rejected it and returned *all seven* violations at once rather than failing on the first. That batching is deliberate: one round-trip tells you everything to fix, instead of a seven-iteration whack-a-mole where each retry burns a hub API call.\r\n- **Two independent objections, not one.** Six findings are Pod Security \"restricted\"-profile requirements; the seventh (`:latest`) is a supply-chain/rollback concern. Fixing `privileged: false` alone still leaves the image objection — they're orthogonal, which is why the guardrail lists them separately.\r\n- **The rejection happened before the human gate, not at it.** The architecture is propose → static+Kyverno v"]
[0.000, "o", "alidation → *then* human approval token via `ocm-mcp approve <id>`. A proposal this bad never gets far enough to consume an operator's attention. That ordering matters: it means the approval step is reserved for judgment calls, not for catching things a linter should have caught.\r\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\r\n\r\n**On the substance:** every one of these is correct to block for a public-facing storefront. `privileged: true` gives the nginx container host-level access — full device access and effectively a container escape path — which a web server serving static content has no need for. And `nginx:latest` means the image running next week may not be the image you tested; there's no fixed artifact to roll back *to*.\r\n\r\n**Nearest thing I can do:** the same storefront, made proposable — `nginx` pinned to a digest, `runAsNonRoot`, `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`, `capabilities.drop: [AL"]
[0.000, "o", "L]`, `seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault`, `automountServiceAccountToken: false`. One caveat you should know before I build it: stock `nginx` binds port 80, which a non-root container can't do — so the compliant version needs `nginx-unprivileged` (listening on 8080) rather than a securityContext tweak alone. That's a real change to what gets deployed, not just a manifest reshuffle.\r\n\r\nWant me to put that version together? And if the storefront genuinely needs privileged access for some reason, tell me what it needs it *for* — that's a policy-exception conversation for your platform team, not something I can route around here.\r\n"]
[0.385, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[0.014, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1;36m# 7. ship it RIGHT - the agent can only PROPOSE, never apply on its own\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[0;90m$ claude -p \"Propose - do not apply - a ManifestWork named storefront on cluster2: a Deployment in namespace ...\"\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[46.225, "o", "**Proposal ID: `102b754367764d29858bf6f5202fc2f1`** — status `pending_approval`. Nothing was applied.\r\n\r\nTo apply it, you mint the token out-of-band and hand it back to me:\r\n\r\n```\r\nocm-mcp approve 102b754367764d29858bf6f5202fc2f1\r\n```\r\n\r\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\r\n- The first attempt was **rejected by static guardrails before Kyverno ever ran** — a cheap deterministic gate ahead of the expensive server-side dry-run. Note the rejection message explains the *why* (\"a credential the workload does not need\"), not just the rule name; that's what lets an agent self-correct in one turn instead of guessing.\r\n- Absence isn't compliance here: `automountServiceAccountToken` defaults to `true`, so the guardrail demands it be **explicitly `false`**. Requiring an explicit deny makes the security posture auditable from the manifest alone rather than inferred from Kubernetes defaults.\r\n`────────────"]
[0.000, "o", "─────────────────────────────────────`\r\n\r\nOne thing to flag before you approve — it passed admission, but it will likely **CrashLoopBackOff at runtime**. The stock `nginx:1.27.4` image starts its master process as root and binds port 80; under `runAsNonRoot` it can't bind a privileged port, and it also needs write access to `/var/cache/nginx` and `/var/run`. The `containerPort: 8080` I set is only metadata — it doesn't change what the image listens on.\r\n\r\nTwo ways to fix it, both needing a fresh proposal (the pending one is immutable):\r\n\r\n1. **Swap the image** to `nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged:1.27.4`, which listens on 8080 as UID 101 by default — the smallest change.\r\n2. **Keep `nginx:1.27.4`** and add a ConfigMap overriding `listen 8080`, plus `emptyDir` mounts for the cache/run paths and an explicit `runAsUser: 101`.\r\n\r\nI'd recommend option 1. Say the word and I'll propose the corrected version; the current proposal can just be l"]
[0.000, "o", "eft to expire unapproved.\r\n"]
[0.389, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[0.013, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1;36m# 8. a human reviews and signs in the terminal (Ed25519) - the only way through\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[0;90m$ ocm-mcp pending\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.511, "o", "  102b754367764d29858bf6f5202fc2f1  cluster=cluster2  kind=manifestwork  name=storefront\r\n          Deploy the storefront workload to cluster2: a 2-replica nginx:1.27.4 Deployment in the shop namespace, hardened to the restricted Pod Security profile (runAsNonRoot, no privilege escalation, all capabilities dropped, RuntimeDefault seccomp) with no service-account token mounted. No Service, Ingress, or RBAC is created; the namespace shop must already exist on the spoke.\r\n"]
[0.077, "o", "\u001b[0;90m$ ocm-mcp show 102b754367764d29858bf6f5202fc2f1\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.065, "o", "id:       102b754367764d29858bf6f5202fc2f1\r\ncluster:  cluster2\r\nname:     storefront\r\nstatus:   pending\r\nsummary:  Deploy the storefront workload to cluster2: a 2-replica nginx:1.27.4 Deployment in the shop namespace, hardened to the restricted Pod Security profile (runAsNonRoot, no privilege escalation, all capabilities dropped, RuntimeDefault seccomp) with no service-account token mounted. No Service, Ingress, or RBAC is created; the namespace shop must already exist on the spoke.\r\nmanifests:\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "[\r\n  {\r\n    \"apiVersion\": \"apps/v1\",\r\n    \"kind\": \"Deployment\",\r\n    \"metadata\": {\r\n      \"labels\": {\r\n        \"app\": \"storefront\"\r\n      },\r\n      \"name\": \"storefront\",\r\n      \"namespace\": \"shop\"\r\n    },\r\n    \"spec\": {\r\n      \"replicas\": 2,\r\n      \"selector\": {\r\n        \"matchLabels\": {\r\n          \"app\": \"storefront\"\r\n        }\r\n      },\r\n      \"template\": {\r\n        \"metadata\": {\r\n          \"labels\": {\r\n            \"app\": \"storefront\"\r\n          }\r\n        },\r\n        \"spec\": {\r\n          \"automountServiceAccountToken\": false,\r\n          \"containers\": [\r\n            {\r\n              \"image\": \"nginx:1.27.4\",\r\n              \"name\": \"nginx\",\r\n              \"ports\": [\r\n                {\r\n                  \"containerPort\": 8080,\r\n                  \"name\": \"http\"\r\n                }\r\n              ],\r\n              \"securityContext\": {\r\n                \"allowPrivilegeEscalation\": false,\r\n                \"capabilities\": {\r\n                  \"drop\": [\r\n                    \"ALL\"\r\n                  ]\r\n                "]
[0.000, "o", "},\r\n                \"runAsNonRoot\": true,\r\n                \"seccompProfile\": {\r\n                  \"type\": \"RuntimeDefault\"\r\n                }\r\n              }\r\n            }\r\n          ],\r\n          \"securityContext\": {\r\n            \"runAsNonRoot\": true,\r\n            \"seccompProfile\": {\r\n              \"type\": \"RuntimeDefault\"\r\n            }\r\n          }\r\n        }\r\n      }\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n]"]
[0.000, "o", "\r\n"]
[0.006, "o", "\u001b[0;90m$ ocm-mcp approve 102b754367764d29858bf6f5202fc2f1 --yes\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.076, "o", "Approval token for this apply (give this to the agent):\r\.Ee7qpXEg0vhuege6PaHScUMSq_a-OWWkgEADAPbl2zALt9ni9x0CDzU9hWLO4ESwPYxuuqVeBD-esLMFlc6dCw\r\n"]
[0.011, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1;36m# 9. only WITH the token can claude apply - then it verifies the rollout\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[0;90m$ claude -p \"Apply proposal 102b754367764d29858bf6f5202fc2f1 on cluster2 using this approval token: eyJhcHByb...\"\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[91.444, "o", "Applied successfully, but **the rollout did not come up**. Here's the accurate picture.\r\n\r\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\r\n- Note the trap this run walked into: `get_manifestwork` reported `Applied=True, Available=True` and would have looked like success. For OCM those conditions mean *the hub delivered the object and it exists on the spoke* — they say nothing about whether the pods run. Deployment readiness is a separate fact, which is why `status_feedback` was empty rather than carrying replica counts.\r\n- The failure also mutated between polls: `ContainerCreating` → `CreateContainerConfigError`. The first sample looked like a benign image pull; only the second revealed a permanent fault. One health check would have produced a wrong answer.\r\n- `CreateContainerConfigError` here is a kubelet *admission* rejection, not a crash. The container never started, so there are no logs to read — `query_events` was the on"]
[0.000, "o", "ly path to the cause.\r\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\r\n\r\n## What happened\r\n\r\nThe apply itself worked — proposal `102b7543…` matched cluster2/`storefront` before I sent the token, and the server returned `applied`.\r\n\r\nThe workload is wedged:\r\n\r\n```\r\nDeployment/storefront (ns shop)   ready 0/2\r\n  storefront-67444754f5-bjn99   Pending   CreateContainerConfigError\r\n  storefront-67444754f5-hkn78   Pending   CreateContainerConfigError\r\n```\r\n\r\nRoot cause, straight from the spoke events:\r\n\r\n> `Error: container has runAsNonRoot and image will run as root (container: nginx)`\r\n\r\nThe proposal hardened the pod to the restricted PSS profile with `runAsNonRoot: true`, but it never set a `runAsUser`. Stock `nginx:1.27.4` declares no non-root `USER` — its entrypoint starts as UID 0 and only drops privileges for worker processes. Kubelet checks the image's configured user at admission, sees root, and ref"]
[0.000, "o", "uses to create the container. The image pulled fine; this is purely the security context contradicting the image.\r\n\r\nThis will not self-heal. It retries indefinitely without ever reaching a container start.\r\n\r\n## What is actually running in `shop` on cluster2\r\n\r\n- **`payments`** — healthy, 2/2, `registry.k8s.io/e2e-test-images/agnhost:2.47`, both containers Started. This was already there and is unaffected.\r\n- **`storefront`** — **not running.** 0/2, both pods stuck pre-start.\r\n\r\nCluster2 itself is fine: hub conditions all True (accepted, joined, available, clock synced), spoke view reachable.\r\n\r\n## To fix\r\n\r\nThe manifest needs one of these — each requires a fresh proposal and a new approval token, since the applied one is now spent:\r\n\r\n1. **Switch to an unprivileged image** — `nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged:1.27-alpine`, built to run as UID 101 and listen on 8080. Cleanest fit with the restricted profile you asked for.\r\n2. **Keep `nginx:1.27.4` and add `runAsUser: 101`** — but this also needs the cont"]
[0.000, "o", "ainer port moved above 1024 and writable `emptyDir` mounts for `/var/cache/nginx` and `/var/run`, or nginx fails at startup instead.\r\n\r\nI'd recommend option 1 — it preserves the hardening intent without bolting on volume workarounds. Want me to draft that as a new `propose_manifestwork`? I'll stop there for you to approve; I won't apply anything without a fresh token.\r\n"]
[0.390, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[0.012, "o", "\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[1;36m# 10. the day, on the record - an operations log straight from the audit trail\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[0;90m$ claude -p \"From the audit trail, summarize this session as an operations log: the fleet review, the placeme...\"\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[105.275, "o", "Read the trail plus the surviving proposal record and the guardrail code that produced the refusals. Here's the log.\r\n\r\n## Operations log — 2026-08-30, 01:29:47–01:33:03 local\r\n\r\n18 audit entries, `seq` 1–18, five separate client processes, one hub (`.demo-connect`).\r\n\r\n**Fleet review** (seq 1–3, 01:29:47)\r\n- `get_addon_health`, `list_clusters`, `list_cluster_management_addons` — all `ok`\r\n- Read-only inventory sweep; no scoping args, whole fleet\r\n\r\n**Placement check** (seq 4–7, 01:30:11)\r\n- `get_placement_decision` on `demo-all` / `default`, then `list_placements`, `list_cluster_set_bindings`, `list_clusters`\r\n- Verified which clusters `demo-all` actually selects, and that the binding backing it exists — before targeting anything\r\n\r\n**Refused shortcut** (seq 9, 01:30:49)\r\n- `propose_manifestwork` — `storefront` → `cluster2`: 1 replica, `nginx:latest`, `securityContext.privileged: true`\r\n- Stated intent in the summary: *\"Requested for a fast ship of a new storefront\"*\r\n- Outcome `rejected` "]
[0.000, "o", "— blocked by static guardrails, never reached a human\r\n- `list_pending_proposals` (seq 10) confirmed nothing was queued by the attempt\r\n\r\n**Second refusal — not in your list, but in the trail** (seq 11, 01:31:40)\r\n- Hardened rewrite: `nginx:1.27.4`, 2 replicas, `runAsNonRoot`, `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`, all caps dropped, `RuntimeDefault` seccomp\r\n- Still `rejected` — `automountServiceAccountToken` was not explicitly `false` (`guardrails.py:146`)\r\n\r\n**Approved change** (seq 12 + 14)\r\n- seq 12, 01:31:47: same manifest plus `automountServiceAccountToken: false` → `ok`, proposal `102b754367764d29858bf6f5202fc2f1` created\r\n- seq 14, 01:32:38: `apply_manifestwork` with approval token (redacted in the audit record) → `ok`, 36 ms\r\n- Proposal record: `status: applied`, `approved_by: sandeepbazar`, `applied_uid 1abb72de-…`\r\n- Token was single-use — `used_tokens.jsonl` shows `jti aeb3c170…` burned at `used_at 1788033758`, ~1h before its `exp`\r\n\r\n**Verification** (seq 15–18, 01:32:42–01:33:03)\r"]
[0.000, "o", "\r\n- `get_manifestwork` cluster2/storefront → `ok`\r\n- `get_cluster_health` cluster2, twice (01:32:47, 01:32:53) → `ok`\r\n- `query_events` cluster2 / `shop`, limit 40 → `ok`\r\n\r\n**Chain integrity**\r\n- `seq` 1–18 unbroken, `prev` on each entry matches the predecessor's `hash` at every link; seq 1 has empty `prev` as expected\r\n- One mutating call total (seq 14); every other entry is a read or a proposal\r\n\r\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\r\n- `classify_outcome` (`tracing.py:302`) is the reason this trail is readable as evidence: tools **return normally** when they refuse, so a naive wrapper would log the privileged-nginx attempt as `ok`. Parsing the `REJECTED`/`FAILED`/`ERROR` prefix out of the return string is what keeps seq 9 and 11 from becoming false successes — and the docstring says explicitly that the eval harness scores from this log.\r\n- The two rejections are more valuable than the approval. They show the "]
[0.000, "o", "gate is *static* — refusing before a human is ever asked — so the operator's attention is only spent on proposals that already pass the floor. `server.py:972` bakes this in, instructing reports to cite rejected proposals \"as evidence the guardrails held.\"\r\n- The second refusal is the interesting one: the agent produced a genuinely hardened spec and was still blocked on one omission. That's a deny-by-default policy rather than a heuristic — `automountServiceAccountToken` must be *explicitly* `false`, since an absent field and a deliberate `false` are indistinguishable to a reviewer skimming YAML.\r\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\r\n\r\nOne caveat on the chain: I verified that each `prev` matches the previous entry's recorded `hash`, which catches truncation or reordering. I did not recompute the hashes from the entry contents, so this doesn't rule out a coordinated rewrite of the whole file "]
[0.000, "o", "— `tests/test_audit_anchor.py` and the anchor mechanism are what cover that.\r\n"]
